## Mandatory canary promotion

`tools/codegen_canary_shared.zig` is the single canary implementation. The
standalone `focused-codegen-canaries` tool and the mandatory integrated-exe
runner both call it directly. The mandatory runner does not invoke the
standalone executable.

The existing integrated-exe inventory remains exactly seven cases, enforced by
a comptime assertion. The existing i128 case now applies the frozen `__multi3`
object, machine-stream, symbol-byte/hash, and six-case executable proof. The
existing fma case now applies the frozen object, machine-stream, symbol-byte,
internal ldexp/add_and_denorm helper, 29-case executable, and no-host-libc-fma
proof. Candidate selection, emitter, environment construction, and the
runner-owned temp root remain owned by the mandatory runner.

The shared validator contains fixed accepted hashes and exposes no expected
hash or runtime override. `assertMandatoryMismatchNegative` deliberately feeds
a mismatch to every fixed identity and requires `CanaryIdentityMismatch`; the
mandatory runner invokes that production function, and the same function has a
direct Zig test. Both the direct shared-validator test and the standalone
focused tool were green before the tested source commit was frozen. The
standalone summary remained two canaries / 35 executable cases / `local_fail=0`.

The mandatory runner summary schema and case count did not change, so no
counter, fixture, normalizer, linker-boundary, distribution-all inventory, or
summary-version migration was triggered. The mandatory contract documentation
was updated in `docs/validation_tiers.md`.

